---
source: tests/stages.rs
expression: reports.report(path)
input_file: tests/fixtures/expand/doc_number_pages.knv
---
(def plus-page "(+ number more ...)\n\nAdds its arguments from left to right.\n\nOne argument returns it, and two emit `a + b`. `+` as a value is the function\nof two numbers, so `(reduce (iter v) 0 +)` sums the items.\n\n(+ 1 2 3)\n=> 6\n\n(reduce (iter (vec 1 2 3)) 0 +)\n=> 6")
(def minus-page "(- number more ...)\n\nSubtracts each later argument from the first.\n\nOne argument negates it with [neg], and two emit `a - b`. `-` as a value is\nthe function of two numbers, and `neg` is unary minus as a value.\n\n(- 10 3 2)\n=> 5\n\n(- 4)\n=> -4")
(def pi-page "pi\n\nHolds the ratio of a circle's circumference to its diameter, as an [f64].\n\nIt emits `std::f64::consts::PI`.\n\n(* 2.0 pi)\n=> 6.283185307179586")
(def number-page "number\n\nMarks a type that does arithmetic.\n\nEvery integer and float type implements it, and [+], [-], [*], [/] and `rem`\ntake its types.\n\n(infer +)\n=> \"(where ((a number)) (fn (a a) a))\"")
(def eq-page "eq\n\nMarks a type whose equality is total, as Rust's `Eq` does.\n\nThe float types leave it out, and a key of a `hash-map` implements it.\n\n(infer into-hash-map)\n=> \"(where ((a hash eq) b) (fn ((vec (pair a b))) (hash-map a b)))\"")
